From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HOTTY Agent <hotty@neuroplast.io>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 16:52:33 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 20/25] renderer: a kitty frame edit uploads only the rectangle
 it changed

A frame edit of the displayed frame (a=f with r) composes a rectangle
into the image in place, but the renderer copied the whole image under
the terminal lock and uploaded it into a new texture, so a one-cell
change to a large image cost the image. Now:

- kitty: the image logs each such edit's rectangle with the generation
  it stamped (Image.changes, freed with the image; markImageRegionChanged
  in the storage). Anything else that stamps a generation breaks the
  chain, and changesSince then says "upload it whole". A full chain
  (64 edits) keeps its latest half, so a renderer a few edits behind
  still finds where it was.
- renderer: when the GPU has an earlier generation and the image knows
  what changed since, only those rectangles are copied under the lock,
  into a new `update` state, and written into the existing texture with
  Texture.replaceRegion.
- OpenGL only (texture_region_updates): GL orders the write after the
  frames that still draw with the texture. Metal's replaceRegion doesn't
  wait for frames in flight, so Metal keeps uploading whole images.

Lines added outside tests: renderer/image.zig 117, graphics_image.zig
95, graphics_exec.zig 11, graphics_storage.zig 15, OpenGL.zig 4,
Metal.zig 5. Tests: the chain and its trimming (graphics_image), an edit logging its
clipped rectangle and a shown frame breaking the chain (graphics_exec),
and the renderer preparing one region (renderer/image).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
